    "## 8. Results & Biological Discussion\n",
    "\n",
    "### Answering the Biological Question\n",
    "1. **Sickle Cell (HbS — Glu6Val):** The mutation causes a radical hydropathy increase ($\\Delta H = +7.7$, Grantham Score $= 121$) and removes the negative charge. Because residue 6 is situated on the solvent-exposed outer face of the A-helix ($< 4\\text{ \\AA}$ to Pro5 and Glu7), this hydrophobic patch does not disrupt monomer folding, but creates an aberrant non-polar contact site that docks into the complementary Phe85/Leu88 pocket of adjacent deoxygenated tetramers.\n",
    "2. **Hemoglobin C (HbC — Glu6Lys):** The mutation causes a $+2$ net formal charge inversion without creating hydrophobicity ($\\Delta H = -0.4$, Grantham Score $= 56$). Lacking the non-polar side-chain required for Phe85/Leu88 docking, Lys6 cannot drive sickle polymerization; instead, altered surface electrostatics promote intraerythrocytic crystal nucleation and solute loss."
